<h2>The recovery and the bail order</h2>
<p>The case arose from FIR No. 4 of 2025, registered at the Anti Narcotic Cell Police Station, North Goa. The police recovered 50.03 grams of cocaine and 15.04 grams of MDMA/Ecstasy from Room No. 106 at Helen Oasis, Vagator — premises run as an Air-B&amp;B style facility. The respondent, according to the prosecution, had been detained in the basement and taken up to the room; when he refused to hand over the keys, duplicate keys were obtained from the facility manager, and the search proceeded in the manager's presence, before panch witnesses, and on video.</p>
<p>The respondent's answer was a frame-up: he said he had actually been picked up two days earlier, tortured — he pointed to a burn on his forearm — and that the contraband was planted in a room he never occupied. The lease, he stressed, stood in the manager's name. The Bombay High Court granted bail, reasoning that the manager had unhindered access to the room, which cast prima facie doubt on whether the respondent had committed the offence.</p>

<h2>Why the Supreme Court interfered</h2>
<p>The State's appeal attacked that single inference, and the Supreme Court accepted the attack. If a manager's alternate keys were enough to disconnect an occupant from a recovery, the Court observed, no recovery from a hotel room could ever be pinned on the person staying in it — management always retains access.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Occupancy was covered.</strong> A leave-and-licence agreement of 1 September 2024 licensed the premises to the manager for commercial use, and the police case — supported by the manager's own statement — was that the room was rented to the respondent from 17 to 26 January 2025, a window that covered both the apprehension and the recovery.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The counter affidavit answered the frame-up plea.</strong> The raiding officer had recorded reliable information on 22 January 2025 that the respondent was staying in Room 106; the seizure was attested by panchas, an Industries Inspector and the manager; and the search was videographed.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The medical point was neutral.</strong> The doctor said the forearm burn could have occurred about two days earlier — but the Court said the larger story of a prior arrest, a bogus raid and planted contraband did not, prima facie, impress it.</div>
</div>
<p>Finding "absolutely no reason to sustain the order granting bail", the Court set aside the High Court's order and directed the respondent to surrender within three weeks of 16 November 2026, failing which he is to be apprehended and produced before the jurisdictional court for judicial custody.</p>

<h2>Setting aside bail versus cancelling bail</h2>
<p>The distinction matters in practice. Cancellation of bail is ordinarily sought for supervening conduct — tampering, absconding, fresh offences. Setting aside a bail order, by contrast, attacks the order itself: the appellate court asks whether the reasoning was legally sustainable when it was passed. Here the Supreme Court did not use the vocabulary of cancellation at all; it simply found the High Court's foundational inference unsound and removed the order from the field.</p>
