<h2>What the decision means for NDPS bail practice</h2>
<p>For the defence, the lesson is that a bail argument in a recovery case must do more than point to third-party access. Where the record places the accused in occupation during the recovery window — bookings, rental statements, prior intelligence, videography — an access-based doubt will rarely survive appellate scrutiny on its own. Arguments about planted contraband, torture or procedural default must be anchored in material that speaks at the prima facie stage, or they will be deferred to trial.</p>
<p>For the prosecution, the case shows the weight a well-prepared counter affidavit carries in the Supreme Court. The Bench relied expressly on the rental period, the manager's statement, the attestation of the seizure and the videography to conclude that the respondent's narrative was demolished for bail purposes.</p>
<div class="note">The observations in a bail appeal are prima facie only. Whether the respondent was in conscious possession of the contraband recovered from Room 106 remains a question for the trial court, unaffected by the Supreme Court's bail-stage findings.</div>
